The underlying set of the finite start set of an expression equals the start set of its automaton.

The proof follows the expression. For the universal language and for a singleton word both sides are the singleton of the deterministic start state. For an intersection the underlying set of a product is the product of the underlying sets, and the inductive hypotheses match the product automaton. For a concatenation the start set is the epsilon-closure of the concatenation automaton's start. If some start state of the first factor is accepting, both directions of the closure law match the union of the left-injected start states of the first factor and the right-injected start states of the second, using a state from that nonempty intersection as the epsilon jump. If no start state of the first factor is accepting, the right-hand disjunct of the closure would exhibit such a state, so only the left-injected start states remain.
